In article <HAIRSTON.90May7102844@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>[aw1s+@andrew.cmu.edu (Andrew David Weiland) writes:]
>[]  It sounds like you just recieved Stormbringer, though i don't know why
>[] it isn't named.  Arioch is the god of chaos ...
>
>that's it! he should have been given Stormbringer but something went
>wrong, a bug perhaps ... this has been mentioned before and i believe
>it was fixed for pl8.

No bug, just a kludge.  Stormbringer already existed somewhere in the
game and that's why Arioch could not grant it.  Look closely at the
message "You have been chosen to ..." and you'll notice a difference
in spelling when the genuine Stormbringer is granted and when not.
Except for the actual "Stormbringer-effects" the sword has the same
attributes and, in fact, a +2 enchantment over Stormbringer to equalize
things a bit.

Now, where was Stormbringer lurking in that game then?  Either unexplored
bones existed somewhere with S in the pile or there was a chaotic priest
in a temple wielding it (temple priests are sometimes granted artifacts).
The latter is no longer an option after PL8, however.

(Presently there exists a slight dilemma regarding unique artifacts, because
when gods grant them, the player should get them too.  The solution I
personally like best was suggested in r.g.h a while back:  Gods steal the
artifact from whoever owns it presently and grant it to the player.  This in
turn sends the offended party in rage looking for the artifact.  This
approach presents certain difficulties, however, and it won't make it to PL8.)

